 "headline": "Luxembourg's trade grew more slowly than its neighbours since 2015 and 2005 but increased its intra-EU trade share, moving closer to the EU while most others moved away.",
 "points": [
  {
   "text": "From 2015 to 2025, Luxembourg had the slowest total trade growth among the eight countries, with its trade index rising to 113 compared to much higher growth in the Netherlands (169), Spain (160), and Italy (158). Despite this, Luxembourg had the highest intra-EU trade share in 2025 at 85.2 %, which increased by 8.0 percentage points since 2015, while six of the other seven countries saw their intra-EU shares fall over this period.",
   "steps": [
    1
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Over the longer run from 2005 to 2025, Luxembourg's total trade index reached 123, again the lowest growth among the group, while the Netherlands and Spain more than doubled their trade values (indices 266 and 221 respectively). Luxembourg's intra-EU trade share rose by 5.0 percentage points to 85.2 %, contrasting with declines in all other countries except Austria, whose share was nearly stable.",
   "steps": [
    2
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Looking at exports alone from 2005 to 2025, Luxembourg's export growth was the slowest with an index of 106, while Spain and the Netherlands had the fastest growth (255 and 268). Luxembourg's intra-EU export share decreased by 6.2 percentage points to 83.1 %, while all other countries' shares also fell except Austria, which fell only slightly by 0.4 points.",
   "steps": [
    3
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "For imports from 2005 to 2025, Luxembourg's growth index was 138, the lowest among the countries, but its intra-EU import share rose substantially by 14.1 percentage points to 86.6 %, the highest share and only notable increase besides Germany's small rise of 1.4 points. Six of the other seven countries saw declines in their intra-EU import shares.",
   "steps": [
    4
   ]
  }
 ],
 "conclusion": "Luxembourg's trade has grown more slowly than Belgium, the Netherlands, Germany, France, Austria, Spain, and Italy since 2005 and 2015. However, Luxembourg stands out by increasing its share of trade within the EU, especially for imports, while most other countries reduced their intra-EU trade shares. This shows Luxembourg is moving closer to the EU in its trade patterns, unlike its neighbours. The data do not explain the reasons behind these trends or their effects on specific sectors or partners, which remain out of scope.",
